"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 6.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pathological stage pT2 without extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of documented nodal sampling widen the uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+3 in both sessions, which is consistent with the prostatectomy grade.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and seminal vesicle invasion. The primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA trajectory and follow-up, as previous notes are missing. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of regional disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
